WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.000
Actae. A refund that survives a crash.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.200
The agent reads the order, records its decision, and calls the payment provider.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.800
At eight seconds the worker is killed — after the refund landed, before the result was recorded.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.500
On restart, the ledger reclaims the operation with a fencing token.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:32.300
The retry presents the same idempotency key, so the provider returns the recorded refund instead of charging again.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.300
In the dashboard, this run shows two attempts of the same payment operation.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:47.500
Attempt two is the reclaim. The inspector shows the key and the original refund.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.500
Now a fork: keep the prefix, change the decision at the boundary.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:11.500
The child run applies a partial refund. The confirmation email is inherited and replayed, never resent.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:29.500
The inspector shows the resolved cursor, the inherited state hash, and the restorable boundary.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
One charge, one email, and a second branch you can compare, then promote.
